From 189440876a09c2687f436db949ceb592c994b3d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Eduardo=20Garc=C3=ADa-M=C3=A1dico=20Portabella?=
 <woodyst@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 11 Jul 2026 16:38:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 002/106] power: supply: add TI BQ25970 switched-capacitor
 charge pump driver

BQ25970 is the fast-charge charge pump on Xiaomi POCO X3 NFC (surya,
SM7150). It's from the same TI family as the already-supported
BQ25980/BQ25975/BQ25960 (drivers/power/supply/bq25980_charger.c), but
its register layout only matches that family for the OVP/OCP threshold
block (0x00-0x09); control, status/fault, and ADC registers are at
different offsets entirely. Extending the shared driver with per-chip
register-address fields would mean an invasive refactor risking the
already-correct 980/975/960 support, so this chip gets its own,
minimal driver instead.

Register map derived from the GPL vendor bq2597x_charger driver used
by Xiaomi's downstream sdmmagpie/surya kernel (TI doesn't publish a
public register-level datasheet for this part). Cross-checked against
a genuine full TI datasheet for the sibling BQ25960 (which does have
one): its real 0x00-0x0E layout matches bq25980_charger.h's assumptions
exactly, confirming both that driver's accuracy and that BQ25970's
control/status block (0x0A onward) genuinely differs, exactly as the
vendor driver's offsets show.

Deliberately registers only a "charger" (Mains-type) power_supply, no
"battery" psy: this chip's VBAT/IBAT would duplicate what the
platform's real fuel gauge (qcom_qg) already reports, and registering
a second POWER_SUPPLY_TYPE_BATTERY device confused UPower's
DisplayDevice merge logic badly enough to show a wrong battery
percentage system-wide on a first attempt that did add one.

TS_BUS/TS_BAT/TDIE protection is disabled in hw_init to match the
vendor board's own proven config (no thermistor pins wired on this
board) - leaving it enabled against floating pins previously produced
a spurious HEALTH_OVERHEAT purely from reading the wrong status
register on this chip via the shared driver's assumed offsets.

Verified on real hardware: chip probes and binds correctly over I2C,
reports sane VBUS/status/health (no more garbage ADC/false overheat).
Not yet doing anything useful power-wise since nothing in this tree
negotiates the Xiaomi HVDCP handshake needed to raise VBUS above 5V,
which this chip requires as a minimum input.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
